            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Mandarin for to scratch, then the Mandarin for to stroke."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: 身上",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "身上 — shēnshang — \"on your person; on one's body\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "我身上没有钱 wǒ shēnshang méiyǒu qián — I haven't got any money on me."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The new character, in pieces: 身 (shēn) — seven strokes: a short fall to the left, a short vertical, a bar that turns down into a long hook, two short bars inside, a long stroke rising to the right, and a long stroke falling to the left. The next lesson puts it in your hand."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "身上 (shēnshang) is built from 身 (shēn) 'body' and 上 'on', both inherited from Middle Chinese, syin and dzyangH (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription), which glosses the first 'oneself' and 'the body'; in the word the second loses its tone."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for describing things."
            }
          ]
        },
